Day 32/120 — I learned that data analysis doesn’t start with data.

It actually starts with a business question.


Today’s lesson was about Business Demand and Business Requests. Before analysts build dashboards or write SQL queries, they first need to understand what the business is trying to solve.

When the request was broken down into user stories, it became clearer what the business needed:

i) To track overall sales performance.
ii) A dashboard to track internet sales performance.
iii) To see which customers buy the most.
iv) They also want to know which products sell the most.

 The next step is designing the data structure...

That’s where I was introduced to Fact Tables and Dimension Tables.

Fact tables store measurable values like sales or revenue.

Dimension tables store descriptive details like customers, products, and dates.

In simple terms:
Facts : the numbers
Dimensions : the context behind the numbers

I’m excited to keep building my SQL skills and continue pushing forward toward Day 120. 🚀
